course: everyday
lesson: 487
title: Money is tight: free school meals and second-hand uniform
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In Sunderland, Dafydd's mum works part-time in a care home and money is tight, so Dafydd gets free school meals. A kid in the queue smirks, «Free dinners, is it?» Dafydd shrugs: «Aye, and they're canny good today, man.» («Canny» in the North East means nice or good.) Inside he's a bit embarrassed, but he's got it right: free school meals are a fair, normal thing that lots of children get. The kid who teases is the one in the wrong.
explain: Lots of families save money on clothes. Nia's school jumper in Swansea came from the uniform swap box and still has «OLIVIA P» in the label, so she scribbles it out (Mum could also sew a new name label in). Second-hand uniform is clean and works just as well, and families who could buy new use the swap box too. In Glasgow, Minnie's dad finds her a disco dress in a charity shop for £3. «It's pure dead brilliant, Dad!» (Glasgow for really brilliant.) Her friend Aimee sniffs, «Whit, a charity shop?» («Whit» is Scots for what.) A good find is a good find, and Aimee's sniff says more about Aimee than the dress.
explain: Sometimes money runs out before payday. Koa in Auckland has togs (that's New Zealand for a swimming costume) that are too small, and swimming starts Monday. Dad says, «Sorry bud, can't swing new ones till payday» («can't swing» means can't afford). Koa thinks about skipping swimming, but schools often keep spare kit and lend it quietly. In Ohio, Jory's sneakers have a hole, so he stuffs cardboard in; in PE it falls out and someone yells, «Ew, sus shoes!» («Sus» means dodgy.) Telling a teacher or counsellor is not begging, and anyone who sees it happen should help, not laugh.
explain: Hiding money troubles can make things worse. Bogdan in Coventry doesn't give Mum the letter about the £35 theme park trip, because he knows she'll worry, and he says he's ill that day. But many schools have money to help with trips, and only a parent can ask, so by hiding the letter Bogdan decided for Mum. Ciaran in Cork tells his friend Nuala «I'm busy» on cinema day when really he's skint (has no money). He could say, «Can't afford it this week. Park instead?» A real friend says yes.
explain: Money can go up and down. Hamish's dad is a fisherman in Inverness: one month it's takeaway twice a week, the next it's beans on toast. «Are we skint again, Da?» «Aye, wee bit, it'll come back.» Islay in Perth, Australia, has had jam sandwiches every day since her dad got hurt at work; she tells a girl, «Yeah nah, it's sweet as» (Aussie for «no, it's fine»), but at home she asks for something different and Mum looks sad. Asking isn't wrong, and a no isn't anyone's fault. And when Amara brings shop biscuits to the cake sale because Mum had no time or eggs, they help just as much as homemade cupcakes.
fact: Free school meals are for families who don't have much money; lots of children get them, and nobody should be teased for it.
fact: Uniform swap boxes and second-hand uniform save families money; the clothes are clean and work just as well.
fact: Charity shops sell good second-hand things cheaply, and the money they make helps a charity; people of every kind shop there.
fact: Schools often keep spare kit, like swimming costumes and PE shoes, and lend it quietly to children who need it.
fact: Many schools have money to help families pay for trips, but a parent has to ask, so the letter should always go home.
fact: School counsellors and charities often help children who need shoes or a coat.
fact: Some jobs, like fishing, pay a lot one month and little the next, so families save in good months for bad ones.
fact: The point of a cake sale is to help, so shop biscuits count just as much as homemade cakes.
fact: «I'm busy» is a common way of hiding money worries; saying «can't afford it this week» and suggesting something free is honest and fine.
fact: It's normal to want something different, and normal for a parent to feel sad when they can't give more; neither is anyone's fault.
fact: If you see someone teased about old shoes or free dinners, the kind thing is not to laugh and to help or tell a teacher.
fact: Words in this lesson: «canny» (North East England) means nice or good, «whit» (Scotland) means what, «pure dead brilliant» (Glasgow) means really brilliant, «togs» (NZ, Australia) are a swimming costume, «can't swing it» means can't afford it, «sus» means suspicious or dodgy, «skint» means out of money, «wee» means small, «yeah nah» means no, «sweet as» means fine.
exercise: Why does Dafydd get free school meals? | His family doesn't have much money | He's the best behaved in his class | His mum works in the school kitchen | He won them in the school raffle
exercise: «Aye, and they're canny good today, man.» What does «canny» mean? | Nice or good | Tinned food | Cold and hard | Not allowed
exercise: Who is in the wrong when a kid teases Dafydd about free dinners? | The kid doing the teasing | Dafydd for taking the dinner | The dinner ladies who served it | Dafydd's mum for being short of money
exercise: Nia's jumper says «OLIVIA P» inside. Where did it come from? | The uniform swap box | Olivia stole it for her | A shop that sells name labels | Her big sister called Olivia
exercise: Why do families use second-hand uniform? | It saves money and works just as well | It's a rule that every pupil must | New uniform is always too big | Shops never sell school jumpers
exercise: How much was Minnie's disco dress from the charity shop? | £3 | £30 | £13 | £35
exercise: «Whit, a charity shop?» What does Aimee's comment really show? | More about Aimee than about the dress | That the dress must be dirty and old | That charity shops are only for the poor | That Minnie should take it back right now
exercise: Koa's togs are too small. What are togs? | A swimming costume | School shoes | A thick winter coat for school | A packed lunch
exercise: «Can't swing new ones till payday.» What does Dad mean? | He can't afford them until he's paid | He can't swim well enough to teach Koa | He can't go to the shops on a weekday | He thinks Koa should stop swimming forever
exercise: What could Koa do instead of skipping swimming? | Ask his teacher quietly for spare togs | Swim in his school uniform instead | Borrow money from a kid in the class | Pretend he's ill every Monday morning
exercise: Someone yells «Ew, sus shoes!» at Jory. What should the other kids do? | Not laugh, and help him | Laugh, it's only a joke | Yell something worse | Film it on a phone
exercise: Why should Bogdan give Mum the trip letter? | The school might help, but only Mum can ask | Mum is cross when letters get lost in bags | The letter says he'll be punished if he doesn't | Every trip is free if you hand it in on time
exercise: What did Ciaran say to Nuala instead of the truth? | «I'm busy» | «I'm ill» | «I'm grounded» | «I hate films»
exercise: What honest thing could Ciaran have said? | «Can't afford it this week. Park instead?» | «Only if you pay for me and my gran too» | «Cinemas are boring, only babies go» | «I'm going to a better cinema with Mum»
exercise: Why does money come and go in Hamish's house? | Fishing pays a lot some months, little in others | Dad gives most of it away to the neighbours | Dad keeps losing his wallet down at the harbour | The bank closes on every second month of the year
exercise: «Yeah nah, it's sweet as.» What does Islay mean? | No, it's fine | Yes, it's sweet | Yes and no, maybe | No, it's horrible
exercise: Amara brings supermarket biscuits to the cake sale. Do they help? | Yes, just as much as homemade cakes | No, only homemade cakes count at sales | Only if she bakes something next time | No, shop biscuits aren't allowed at school
test: «free dinners r for scroungers lol». Is that right? | No, they're a fair help lots of families get | Yes, families should pay for everything | Yes, only lazy families ever need them | No, because dinners cost nothing to cook
test: Nia scribbled out OLIVIA P because she didn't want anyone to see. What's another fix the lesson gives? | Ask Mum to sew a new label in | Throw the jumper in the bin | Give it back to Olivia's house | Write OLIVIA on her bag too
test: In a cartoon, the girl in the charity-shop dress gets laughed off the dance floor. What's more real? | Plenty of people shop there and nobody can tell | Charity-shop clothes always fall apart on you | Only grown-ups are allowed in charity shops | Charity shops sell nothing but old curtains
test: What would most likely happen if Koa told his teacher about his togs? | The school would lend him some, no fuss | He'd be told off for not buying new ones | He'd be banned from the swimming term | The teacher would phone his dad to shout
test: Bogdan: «didn't give mum the letter, the trip's £35, she'd only stress». What did he get wrong? | He chose for Mum, and the school might have helped | He should have forged Mum's signature on the slip | He should have paid the £35 out of his own savings | Nothing, hiding letters is always the kind thing
test: Jory: «shut up, they're my lucky shoes, the cardboard's on purpose». Why might he say that? | He's embarrassed and hiding that money is short | He really believes cardboard is lucky in PE | He wants everybody to copy his shoes tomorrow | He's showing off his brand-new running trainers
test: Roisin has no £1 for non-uniform day, and her own clothes are her brother's old hoodie. She wears her uniform and says she forgot. What else could she do? | Quietly tell the teacher she can't do it this time | Take a pound from her mum's purse without asking | Stay at home that day and pretend to be poorly | Borrow a posh outfit from a girl she hardly knows
test: Hamish's dad caught loads in June and almost nothing in July. What would a wise family do in June? | Save some for the bad months | Spend it all on takeaways and treats | Buy a second fishing boat | Stop fishing in July
test: Islay asked Mum for something different and Mum looked sad. «I'm a horrible kid for asking.» True? | No, asking is normal, and Mum's sadness isn't her fault | Yes, kids should never ask for anything at all | Yes, she made Mum sad on purpose with that | No, but she must eat jam forever to make up
test: Six kids bring cupcakes and Amara brings a pack of 20 biscuits. Cupcakes sell at 50p and biscuits at 10p. If six cupcakes and all the biscuits sell, which raised more? | The six cupcakes, by a pound | The biscuits, by a pound | They raised exactly the same | The biscuits, by two pounds
test: Nuala says «cinema Saturday?» and Ciaran's skint. Which reply is honest and keeps the friendship? | «Can't this week. Kickabout in the park?» | «Busy. Every single Saturday, for ever, sorry.» | «Ask me again when I'm rich, OK?» | «No, I don't even like you, Nuala.»
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises free school meals? | A fair provision for families of limited means, received by many pupils | A reward conferred solely upon pupils of exemplary conduct and attainment | A privilege reserved for the children of staff employed by the school | A penalty imposed upon families who have failed to settle their debts
exam: Given that a pupil withholds from his parent a letter concerning a school excursion on the grounds of cost, identify the principal error. | He denied the parent the chance to request help | He failed to forge the parent's signature | He neglected to pay the sum from his savings | He should have destroyed the letter at once
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning garments acquired second-hand. | They are clean, serviceable and widely used | They are prohibited under school regulations | They are worn only by families without income | They are invariably unclean and unserviceable
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate course for a pupil whose swimming costume no longer fits and whose parent cannot purchase another until payday. | To inform a teacher, who may lend spare kit | To absent himself from every swimming lesson | To borrow money from a classmate for a new one | To swim in his school uniform without asking
